content.text:
Done. The change makes the "Mark as Completed" button stay disabled in the too-many-datapoints condition.

Why your version broke: `exceedsUpsertLimit` was derived from `selectedDeliveries.length`, but `handleSubmitDialog` runs `setSelectedDeliveries([])` immediately after the upsert — so when the backend rejects the 101-item batch, the selection empties, `exceedsUpsertLimit` flips to `false`, and the button re-enables.

The fix bases the check on `receivableDeliveriesCount` (deliveries still `in_progress`), which reflects what the batch would actually send and doesn't change when the selection is cleared:

```tsx
const receivableDeliveriesCount =
  supplyDeliveries?.results?.filter(
    (delivery) => delivery.status === SupplyDeliveryStatus.in_progress,
  ).length ?? 0;

const exceedsUpsertLimit =
  receivableDeliveriesCount > careConfig.maxDatapointsPerUpsert;
```

The existing `disabled={... || exceedsUpsertLimit}` on the Mark as Completed button now stays `true` as long as there are more receivable deliveries than the batch limit, regardless of selection state.

One thing to flag: with >100 in-progress deliveries, the "Receive & Update Stock" batch will keep failing since it sends them all at once — so the order can't progress at all. If you want that path to actually work, the real fix is to chunk the upsert into batches of `maxDatapointsPerUpsert`. Want me to add chunking to the receive/update handlers?

